      "textEn": "Whatever is related to me, that is mine. 7. Meaning of the word\n\nNityasya—Nitya; Arthasya—of the object; Sambandhah—relationship; Hi—indeed; Nityah—indeed, momentary; Drishyate—is seen; Nrikshu—in human society; Yavat—until; Yasya—whose; Hi—indeed, without doubt; Sambandhah—relationship; Mamatvam—\nAttachment, affection; Tavat—until; Ev—indeed; Hi—certainly; Nishchayah—certainly. Only a few souls are born in the human womb, and the rest are born in other animal wombs. Although these two are souls, their relationship is temporary. An animal can remain in the possession of a human for some time and then go into the possession of another. When an animal\nis sold, the ownership of the previous owner also goes away. As long as that animal remains in his\nauthority, he has affection for it, but as soon as he sells it, all affection\ndisappears.\nMeaning: It is clear from the example given in this verse that the transformation of the soul from one body to another, along with the transformation, the relationship between the souls in this life is also temporary. Chitraketu's son's name was Harshashoka or Harsha and Shoka. The soul is certainly eternal, but its eternality is not revealed by being covered by the inauspicious attire of the body. In this body, as it is, the body is like a child.\nYouth and old age—\"With this body, the soul continuously goes through childhood, youth, and then old age.\"\nIt keeps going.” Thus, this physical attire is temporary. But the soul is permanent. In the same way that an animal goes from one owner to another. Similarly, the soul, which was for some time\nin the form of the son of Chitraketu, when it entered another body, all affection and relationships broke\nup. As it is said in the example given in the previous verse, when any commercial material remains in someone's hand, it belongs to him, but as soon as it reaches another's hand, it becomes someone else's.\nThen, neither does he have any relationship with him, nor 