      "textEn": "Whatever is attainable, that is his own self. 8. Meaning of the word\n\nEvam—in this way; Yoni-gatah—going into a particular womb; Jivah—soul; Sahah—that; Nityah—eternal, perpetual;\nNirahankatah—without ego; Yavat—until; Yatra—where; Upalabhyete—that can be found; Tavat—until;\nSwatvam—knowledge of oneself; Hi—indeed; Tasya—his; Tat—that. Even though one soul may seem to be connected to another soul due to the relationships of mortal bodies,\nthe soul is eternal. In reality, the body is born and destroyed, the soul\nis not. We should not assume that the soul is born or dies. The soul has no relation to\nso-called parents. Until the soul, as a result of its past deeds,\nappears as a son to some parents, until then, it remains connected to the body given by the parents. In this way, considering himself as their son,\nhe shows excessive affection. This relationship is destroyed after death.\nIn such circumstances,\na person should not be absorbed in false joy and sorrow. Meaning: When the soul resides within the body, it considers itself to be a false body.\nAlthough, in reality, it is not so. His relationship with his body and so-called parents\nremains false, this is delusion. Until someone is aware of the reality of the soul,\nthe human being remains trapped in this delusion. He is eternal, unchanging, subtle, and the support of all. The Lord creates the universe and the self in the form of the self-illusion. 9. Meaning of the word\n\nEshah - this soul; Nityah - eternal, perpetual; Avyayah - imperishable; Sukshmah - subtle (not visible to the eye);\nEshah - this soul; Sarva-ashrayah - the cause of various types of bodies; Swa-drk - self-radiance; Atma-maya-gunaih -\nBy the qualities of the illusion of Shri Bhagavan; Vishvam - in this material world; Atmanam - oneself; Srujate - manifests;\nPrabhuh - the Lord. The soul is eternal and indestructible because it has no beginning or end.\nNeither is his\nbirth, nor death. He is the origin of all kinds of bodies, yet in his physical class"
