      "textEn": "Counting doesn’t matter. The soul is so high that it is of the same religion as God. Even then, being extremely\nsubtle, he remains fascinated by the external power, and according to his wishes,\nhe creates various bodies for himself. Meaning: This verse describes the philosophy of non-dualistic non-differentiation. The soul is the complete Supreme Being.\nIt is eternally equal to God; the only difference is that the Supreme Being is immense, and there is no one greater than Him.\nNor is there anyone equal to Him, while the soul is subtle. The scriptures say that the soul is equal to the tenth-thousandth part of the tip of the hair. The Supreme Being is all-pervading (Antarsthit-aparatman-\nChayantarastam). If, relatively, the soul is considered subtle (very small), then the question arises, who is the greatest (Virat)? Virat is Shri Bhagwan, and the soul is very\nsmall. The second characteristic of the Jiva is that it becomes covered by Maya. By the qualities of self-illusion—He\nis to be enveloped by the illusion of Shri Bhagavan.\nThe soul itself is responsible for the bound life in this world,\nTherefore, it is called the Lord (Swami). If he wants, he can come into this world.\nAnd if he wants, he can go back to the abode of God. Because he wants to enjoy the pleasures of the physical world,\ntherefore, Shri Bhagwan has given him a physical body through Maya. Bhagavan\nKrishna himself has said in the Bhagavad Gita (18.61)—\n\nIshwarah sarvabhutanam hridayesha arjuna tishthati. The senses, which are situated in the instruments, are controlled by Maya. “Hey Arjun! God is in the heart of every creature. He is enshrining all beings in the body-like instrument,\nand is rotating them with his power of illusion.\"The Supreme Being provides the soul with the opportunity to\nenjoy the pleasures of this world according to its wishes, but at the same time, God expresses His will\nto renounce all desires, surrender them, and return to the Supreme Abode"
