      "textEn": "Go. The soul is very subtle. In this context, Jiva Goswami states that for material scientists, finding the soul in the body is extremely difficult, although we know from scholars\nthat the soul is within the body. The body is separate from the soul. Neither pleasures nor pains, nor is he dear to anyone, nor is he dear to himself. One sees all the actions, the doer, and the qualities and defects. 10. Meaning of the word\n\nN—not; H—indeed; Asy—of the soul; Asti—is; Priyah—beloved; Kashchit—anyone; Na—not; Apriyah—\nUnpleasant; Swah—own; Parah—other; Api—also; Va—or; Ekah—one; Sarvadhiyam—of the different types of intellect; Drashta—seeing; Kartranam—of the doers; Gunadhoyoh—of virtues and faults, of right and wrong; Yuktah—with proper and improper; Anu—like; Anu—like; Guna-dosha-yoh—of virtues and faults, of right and wrong. This soul has neither friends nor enemies. This does not discriminate against its own. This is unique, meaning it is neither influenced by friends and enemies, nor by well-wishers or those who are stubborn. This is merely a spectator or witness to the various qualities of humans.\nMeaning: As stated in the previous verse, the qualities of the soul and Shri Bhagavan are one,\nbut because the soul is subtle, it keeps them in a smaller quantity, while the Supreme Being is omnipresent and\nimmense. The Supreme Being is neither a friend, nor an enemy, nor a relative, because He is beyond all kinds of inabilities of bound souls. On the other hand, they remain extremely kind and compassionate towards their devotees. They are not at all pleased with those who are jealous of their devotees. In the Bhagavad Gita,\n(9.29) God himself has confirmed this—\nI am not hateful to all beings, nor am I dear. [^1]: They worship me with devotion, and I am pleased with them. [^2]: “I neither hate anyone nor am I partial to anyone; my attitude is equanimity towards all living beings.”"
