      "textEn": "It is. But those creatures who serve me with devotion, they are my friends and are situated within me, and I\nam also their friend.\" God is neither enemy nor friend, but He is favorable to that devotee who\n is always engaged in His devotion. Similarly, in the Gita (16.19), the Lord says elsewhere—\n\nTanaham dvishatah kruranasansehushu naradhamaan. Kshipamyajasramshubhanasurishve yonishu. “Those who are jealous and stubborn, I constantly throw those vile beings into the ocean of existence, into demonic wombs.\nI drop them.” Gods are against them, who hate their devotees. For the protection of his devotees,\nSometimes God has to kill their enemies as well. For example, Prahlad\nTo protect the king, he had to kill his enemy Hiranyakashipu, although by being killed by him,\nHe attained salvation. Because God is the witness to everyone's actions, therefore he keeps watching the actions of his devotees'\nenemies and remains ready to punish them.\nBut other\nHe remains a witness to the actions of living beings and gives them fruits according to sin or virtuous deeds.\nHe gives. The soul is neither of qualities nor of non-quality, nor is it the result of action. Udasinavadasinah Paravarahagishwarah. 11. Meaning of the word\n\nNa—No; Adatta—Accepts; Atma—Soul; Hi—Certainly; Gunam—Sweetness; Na—No; Dosham—Sin; Na—No\nThen; Kriya-phalam—The fruit of any action; Udasin-vat—Like a detached person; Asinah—Sitting (in the heart); Para-\nAvar-drk—Looking at the work and the cause; Ishwarah—God. The destroyer of action and cause, this soul does not accept the happiness and sorrow caused by desirable actions. He is completely free to accept or not accept the physical body, and\nbecause he does not have a physical body, he always remains indifferent or neutral. The soul is a different part of God\nand in subtle quantity, it retains their qualities. Therefore, man is sorrowful"
